 and making lunch and  like bathing children I don't know how  we got anything I knew John was it was a  great writing partner when he's offering  dialogue suggestions while giving one of  my kids a horsey ride  either we acted fast just to make it in  time for this year or we since Matt  worked the summertime we shot later and  the film would come out like a year and  a half later or next next season and  because of the topical nature of the  issue we thought it was better sooner  and it was plenty of time it was I think  plenty of time for guests it's like well  week scheduled for format and then Gus  took a while I was the budget and he was  like yeah I'll shoot it in six weeks  more like when Warner's was backing the  movie I remember having conversations  saying you know guys I mean I need at  least 45 days to do this thing are you  kidding me i mean you know Gus did it in  30 you know one of the big benefits for  me was that I prepared it as a director  and and then got to watch the decisions  that Gus made and there were things that  he did just like on Good Will Hunting  that you know we hadn't thought of when  we wrote it and and one of them on the  second day one of one of my favorite  shots in the film happened on day two of  shooting which was the first day that I  went to pitch the lease to Tim guineas  character and Tim Tim was the first  farmer that I meet he's got the  beautiful little daughter with the  golden curly hair and and and we go into  his house and I start to pitch this  lease to him and and we got in there and  Gus took the little girl and she was  like six or seven years old and gave her  a coloring book and Satre at the kitchen  table and like any kid I mean it's just  great because a kid that age gets a task  and they just start you know they  diligently said about doing it she  starts coloring in the coloring book and  she just forgot about the camera and we  were staged in the other room so so  she's very prominent in the foreground 